**Read:** [University Physics Volume 1 — OpenStax](../sources/University-Physics-Volume-1.lingua#r_2W7QCEHZ45XDB3AWVG9NTV36F6), hydrostatic pressure and its applications; [Introdução à Mecânica dos Fluidos e aos Fenômenos de Transporte — Gobbi and colleagues](../sources/Mecanica-dos-Fluidos-e-Transportes-Gobbi-Dias.lingua#r_1F9KHVCP86X68RR7DTZASE5F1J), chapter 4 hydrostatics; [Fluid Mechanics — Richard Fitzpatrick](../sources/Analytical-Fluid-Mechanics-Fitzpatrick.lingua#r_075CGXNA8WHV26ZW53C7XTQ48V), hydrostatic pressure and compressible equilibrium sections.

Balance the forces on a small stationary fluid element. With gravity vector g, equilibrium requires ∇p=ρg. For a vertical coordinate z increasing upward and constant gravitational magnitude, dp/dz=−ρg. For constant density, a depth h below a surface adds ρgh to its pressure.

For an illustrative liquid of density 1,000 kg/m³ with g=9.81 m/s², a depth of 2 m adds 19,620 Pa. State the surface pressure separately. If density varies with pressure, temperature or composition, integrate the actual relation instead of replacing it with one unexplained average. A static ideal-gas column also needs a temperature model.

Draw the pressure distribution and explain why container shape does not appear in the local balance. The total support force still depends on the surfaces and their orientation. Use a pressure reading at one height to predict another and identify which assumptions a disagreement would test.

